        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The best charcoal grill for most people is still a 22-inch Weber kettle. It sears, roasts and smokes well, it is easy to learn on and it costs a fraction of a premium grill. Buy a kamado only if you want to smoke for hours at a time and are happy with the weight and price that come with heavy insulation. Everything else on this page is a variation on those two ideas, and the right choice depends far more on how you cook than on which brand you pick.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Chose
          </h2>

          <p>
            A charcoal grill is one of the simplest things you can buy for a backyard, which is exactly why the category is full of grills that look the same and cook very differently. A thin, leaky bowl burns through charcoal and swings in temperature every time the wind changes. A well-sealed kettle or kamado holds heat steadily, uses less fuel and gives you real control over whether you are searing at 600 degrees or smoking at 250. So we did not start from a list of bestsellers; we started from what makes charcoal cooking easy or frustrating, and looked for the grills that get those basics right.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every grill on this page is a real, currently sold model, and every figure we quote comes from the manufacturer's spec sheet or a retailer listing, attributed where we use it. We did not lab-test these grills, and we do not publish scores we did not measure. Where listings disagreed, as they did on weights and some dimensions, we say so on the card. Prices move constantly in this category, especially around spring and holiday sales, so treat any price as a snapshot and check the listing you buy from.
          </p>

          <p>
            We weighted four things above everything else. First, how well the grill holds and adjusts temperature, which comes down to how it is sealed, insulated and vented. Second, cooking area in practical terms: whether the grate fits the food you cook for the people you cook for. Third, how easy it is to light, add fuel to and clean, because a grill that is a chore gets used less. Fourth, durability and support: the material of the bowl, the warranty and how easy it is to buy parts five years from now.
          </p>

          <p>
            That left two clear families. Kettles, led by Weber's 22-inch designs, are the most versatile and forgiving way into charcoal cooking. Kamados, ceramic or insulated steel, cost more and weigh more but hold low temperatures for many hours on a single load of fuel. We picked the strongest options in each, plus one budget kamado for people who want to try the style without the investment.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Kettle, Kamado or Barrel: The Three Shapes
          </h2>

          <p>
            A kettle grill is a round bowl with a domed lid and adjustable vents top and bottom. The shape reflects heat back onto the food, so it roasts as well as it grills, and the vents give enough control for two-zone cooking: coals banked on one side for searing, food on the other side for gentle indirect heat. Kettles are light, relatively cheap and forgiving, and a 22-inch kettle is big enough for most families. Their limitation is insulation. A thin steel bowl loses heat in wind and cold, so long smokes need more fuel and more attention.
          </p>

          <p>
            A kamado is a thick-walled egg-shaped cooker, traditionally ceramic, that seals tightly and insulates heavily. That combination means very little air gets in, so the charcoal burns slowly and the temperature barely moves once it settles. Retailers list the Kamado Joe Classic III at up to 12 hours of low-and-slow cooking on one load. The same insulation that holds 225 degrees overnight can also reach 700 degrees and more for searing. The drawbacks are weight, price and patience: a ceramic kamado can weigh 160 to 280 pounds and takes longer to come down if you overshoot your target temperature.
          </p>

          <p>
            Barrel grills, the long horizontal drums with a chimney, give the most grate area per dollar and suit people who cook large amounts of food at once. They are the least efficient with fuel and the least precise, and many are better thought of as budget smokers. If you mainly want to smoke, our separate guides to the <a href="/best-smoker/">best smokers</a> and <a href="/best-electric-smoker/">best electric smokers</a> cover offset, pellet, charcoal and electric options in more depth.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which Shape Should You Buy?</h3>

          <p>
            If you grill burgers, chicken and steaks most weekends and smoke occasionally, buy a 22-inch kettle. If you want to smoke briskets and pork shoulders regularly and are happy to spend more, buy a kamado. If you cook for crowds and value space over precision, a barrel or a larger grill is worth a look. Most people who own a kamado still say a kettle is the better first grill, because it teaches fire control quickly and costs a fraction as much.
          </p>
        </div>

