          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best charcoal grill for beginners?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 22-inch kettle such as the Weber Original Kettle Premium is the easiest place to start. It is forgiving, lights easily with a chimney starter, handles both direct and indirect cooking, and costs far less than a kamado. Most people learn two-zone fire control on it within a few cooks.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a kamado better than a kettle grill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A kamado is better at holding low temperatures for many hours with very little fuel, which makes it excellent for smoking. A kettle is lighter, cheaper, faster to adjust and better as an everyday grill. If you mainly grill and occasionally smoke, a kettle is the better value.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How big a charcoal grill do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a family of four to six, a 22-inch kettle with about 360 square inches of primary grate is enough. Smaller households can use 14 to 18 inches, while frequent entertainers should look at 24 inches or more, remembering that two-zone cooking halves the direct-heat area.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I use briquettes or lump charcoal?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Briquettes burn evenly and predictably, which suits kettles and long cooks. Lump charcoal lights faster, burns hotter and leaves less ash, which suits kamados. Check your grill's manual, because many kamado makers recommend lump charcoal specifically.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a charcoal grill last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A porcelain-enameled kettle kept covered and cleaned can last many years, and ceramic kamados commonly carry limited lifetime warranties on their ceramic parts. Thin, uncoated steel grills left in the rain may rust out in a few seasons.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a charcoal grill on a balcony or in a garage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Never use a charcoal grill in a garage or any enclosed space, because it produces carbon monoxide. Many local fire codes also restrict charcoal grills on apartment balconies, so check your building and local rules before buying one.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is gas ignition on a charcoal grill worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For weeknight cooking it is genuinely convenient, because it lights the coals at the push of a button without a chimney or fluid. It adds cost and needs disposable propane cylinders, so it is a convenience upgrade rather than a necessity.
              </p>
            </div>

